-names>PC</given-names></name><name><surname>Bombardier</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The outcome of patients with rheumatoid arthritis who were referred to received treatment from a primary therapist &#x02013; A randomized controlled trial</article-title><source>Arthritis Rheum</source><year>2006</year><volume>55</volume><fpage>42</fpage><lpage>52</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16463410</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/art.21692</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B6"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stokes</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name><name><surname>Helewa</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lineker</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Total assessment of rheumatoid polyarthritis&#x02013;a postgraduate training program for physical and occupational therapists: a 20 year success story</article-title><source>J Rheumatol</source><year>1997</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>1634</fpage><lpage>1638</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9263163</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lundon</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Shupak</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Sunstrum-Mann</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Galet</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Schneider</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Leading change in the transformation of arthritis care: Development of an inter-professional academic-clinical education training model</article-title><source>Healthcare Quarterly</source><year>2008</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>59</fpage><lpage>65</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Campos</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Graveline</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lundon</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Feldman</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Schneider</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>The physical therapy practitioner (PTP) in pediatric rheumatology: high level of patient and parent satisfaction with services</article-title><source>Physiother Can</source><year>2002</year><volume>54</volume><fpage>32</fpage><lpage>36</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Campos</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Graveline</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lundon</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Schneider</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Laxer</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The physical therapy practitioner: an expanded role for physical therapy in pediatric rheumatology</article-title><source>Physiother Can</source><year>2001</year><volume>53</volume><fpage>282</fpage><lpage>287</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Robarts</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Kennedy</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>MacLeod</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name><name><surname>Findlay</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Gollish</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A framework for the development and implementation of advanced practice roles for physiotherapists that improves access and quality of care for patients</article-title><source>Healthcare Quarterly</source><year>2008</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>75</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18362523</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>The Chartered Society of Physiotherapy</collab></person-group><source>Chartered Physiotherapists Working as Extended Scope Practitioners (ESP): Guidance for Members</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>London, UK, The Chartered Society of Physiotherapy</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shipton</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Badley</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name><name><surname>Mahomed</surname><given-names>NN</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Critical shortage of orthopaedic services in Ontario, Canada</article-title><source>Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery</source><year>2003</year><volume>85</volume><fpage>1710</fpage><lpage>1715</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12954829</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hochberg</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Early aggressive DMARD therapy: the key to slowing disease progression in rheumatoid arthritis</article-title><source>Scand J Rheumatol Suppl</source><year>1999</year><volume>112</volume><fpage>3</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10668521</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1080/030097499750042227</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Quinn</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Conaghan</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name><name><surname>Emery</surname><given-na